          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#SolutionSpaceOfPointExtensions Point-extensions
          %p
            Of particular interest is the special case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            to a single point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in \hat X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \hat X \setminus \{p\}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of that point.
          %p
            Contrary to ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+functions"} generalized functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+with+point+support"} distribution with point support
            at that point, hence some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative+of+a+distribution"} derivative
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+distribution"} delta distribution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support+of+a+distribution"} supported
            at that point.
          %p
            However, if one requires that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+degree+of+a+distribution"} scaling degree
            of the extended distribution at the given point is compatible with that of the original distribution, then there is only a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            of possible extensions, parameterized by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            of a finite number of derivatives of the delta-distribution (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SpaceOfPointExtensions"}
            below).
          %p
            In the construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbative quantum field theories
            via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            coefficients in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+matrix"} scattering matrix
            and where the point being extended to corresponds to the point where an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            happens, this finite set of choices is identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re"-)normalization
            freedom.
          %p
            We discuss specifically the space of solutions of extending a distribution on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \setminus \{0\}
            of the origin inside a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            to the full space
